  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list><item>Frequent disabling headaches on about 20 days a month, with nausea, vomiting and light sensitivity, mostly right-sided, since childhood and more frequent over the past year, with a combination headache tablet containing butalbital taken on about 16 days a month; under evaluation with a headache diary, MRI, syphilis serology and a headache medicine consultation: active, confirmed [S]</item><item>High myopia in both eyes, much worse in the right eye, since early childhood, with long-standing poor vision in the right eye and unequal pupils; comprehensive eye examination booked: active, confirmed [S]</item><item>Chronic insomnia, with difficulty falling asleep and early waking, worse on headache nights; no sleep medicine at intake: active, confirmed [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Diagnosis: a structured headache history, a red-flag screen (SNNOOP10) at every visit, a 14-day diary of headache days, attack features, triggers and every dose of acute medicine, and ICHD-3 criteria; MRI of the brain with and without contrast because the headaches have become more frequent over the past year, are strongly right-sided, and come with long-standing unequal pupils and a very poor right eye; treponemal and nontreponemal syphilis serology because of past gonorrhea, unequal pupils and poor vision. Guideline: Headache Classification Committee of the International Headache Society (IHS). The International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition (2018); The American Headache Society Consensus Statement: Update on integrating new migraine treatments into clinical practice (2021); Choosing wisely in headache medicine: the American Headache Society&apos;s list of five things physicians and patients should question (2013); Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines, 2021 (2021) [S]</item><item>Neurology (headache medicine) consultation to confirm the diagnosis and advise on withdrawal of the overused tablets and on preventive treatment. Guideline: The American Headache Society Consensus Statement: Update on integrating new migraine treatments into clinical practice (2021); Calcitonin gene-related peptide-targeting therapies are a first-line option for the prevention of migraine: An American Headache Society position statement update (2024); European Academy of Neurology guideline on the management of medication-overuse headache (2020) [S]</item><item>Medication overuse at intake: explain how frequent combination tablets can keep headaches going; count every tablet and acute-medicine day in the diary; take no more than now and never more than four combination tablets a day until the consultation; no new sedatives or sleeping tablets. Guideline: Headache Classification Committee of the International Headache Society (IHS). The International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition (2018); European Academy of Neurology guideline on the management of medication-overuse headache (2020) [S]</item><item>Comprehensive eye examination for high myopia: refraction, pupils, pressure, a dilated fundus examination for myopic degeneration, retinal breaks and lattice, and macular OCT; correction of the large difference between the eyes; the warning signs of retinal detachment and an Amsler grid; dilated examination every year. Guideline: Refractive Errors Preferred Practice Pattern (2023); Posterior Vitreous Detachment, Retinal Breaks, and Lattice Degeneration Preferred Practice Pattern (2025); Comprehensive Adult Medical Eye Evaluation Preferred Practice Pattern (2026) [S]</item><item>Sleep: cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia through the clinic&apos;s behavioral sleep program, a fixed rising time, a wind-down hour, and no sedative sleeping tablets. Guideline: Behavioral and psychological treatments for chronic insomnia disorder in adults: an American Academy of Sleep Medicine clinical practice guideline (2021) [S]</item><item>Mood: PHQ-9 and GAD-7 at intake and at about two to three months, with direct questions about hopelessness and self-harm at each visit; a safety assessment if a score or answer calls for one. Guideline: CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026); Depression and Suicide Risk in Adults: Screening (2023) [S]</item><item>Reading, writing and walking: good light, large print and an enlarged screen, breaks every 30 minutes, voice dictation for long passages, sunglasses and a brimmed hat for glare, and his daily walks kept as a protected part of the plan. Guideline: The American Headache Society Consensus Statement: Update on integrating new migraine treatments into clinical practice (2021); Refractive Errors Preferred Practice Pattern (2023) [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
